.TH IDO-QUEUE 5 "2026-10-14" "ido 0.1.0-beta.1" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
ido-queue \- the queue file read by ido
.SH LOCATION
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/ido/queue.jsonl
(default
.IR ~/.local/state/ido/queue.jsonl ).
Under state rather than runtime, so a queue survives a logout.
The lock is
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/ido/lock ,
or a directory under state when that variable is unset.
Nothing is ever placed under
.IR /tmp .
.PP
One queue per user.
There is no shared queue: a shared queue is a way for one user to get another
user to run their command.
.SH PERMISSIONS
The directory is created
.B 0700
and the file
.BR 0600 .
.B ido
refuses to read a queue directory or file that belongs to another user or is
writable by group or world, checked on the opened descriptor.
.SH FORMAT
One JSON object per line.
A line this version cannot parse refuses the whole file; unknown keys are an
error, never ignored.
.TP
.B schema_version
Always 1 in this version.
.TP
.B id
Monotonic, unique within the file's history.
.TP
.B argv
The command, as an array of strings, executed exactly as stored with no shell.
.TP
.BR reason ", " group ", " step ", " needs ", " cwd ", " dry_run_of
As given to
.BR "ido queue" .
All are untrusted text and are sanitized before display.
.TP
.B queued_at
Unix seconds.
.TP
.B queued_by
Who queued it, by the caller's own claim; shown as unverified.
.TP
.BR attempts ", " last_exit
Set when a run fails.
A successful run removes the entry.
.PP
Status is never stored.
Whether an entry is ready, blocked, halted, failed, or stale is derived from
the queue each time it is read, so it cannot drift from the facts.
An entry older than seven days is shown as stale and is never removed
automatically;
.B ido drop \-\-stale
removes stale entries.
.SH WRITING
Queueing appends one line under the lock.
Every other change writes a temporary file beside the queue, syncs it, and
renames it into place.
The lock is held for milliseconds and never while a command runs.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ido (1)
